.TH SEQ 1 2026-09-29 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
seq \- print a sequence of numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B seq
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR format ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR string ]
.RI [ first
.RI [ incr ]]
.I last
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B seq
prints the numbers from
.I first
to
.I last
in steps of
.IR incr ,
one per line.
.I first
and
.I incr
default to 1.
With a negative
.I incr
the sequence counts down; if
.I last
cannot be reached (for example
.B seq 5 1
with the default increment), nothing is printed.
.PP
The numbers may have a fractional part and may be negative.
Each value is computed as
.IR first " + " n " \(mu " incr ,
so steps such as 0.1 do not accumulate rounding error, and a last value
within rounding of
.I last
is included.
By default each number is printed with as many decimal places as
.I first
or
.I incr
was written with, so
.B seq 0 0.25 1
prints 0.00, 0.25, ... 1.00.
.PP
An argument that looks like a negative number (such as
.BR \-3 )
is an operand, not an option.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " format"
Print each number with the
.BR printf (3)
.IR format ,
which must contain exactly one floating-point conversion
.RB ( %e ,
.BR %f ,
.BR %g ,
or their capitals, with optional flags, width and precision) and may
contain
.B %%
and other text.
.TP
.BI \-s " string"
Separate the numbers with
.I string
instead of a newline.
A newline still ends the output.
.TP
.B \-w
Pad the numbers with leading zeros, after any minus sign, so all have the
same width.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The sequence was printed (possibly empty).
.TP
.B 1
An operand was not a number, the increment was zero, the format was
invalid, or the arguments were wrong.
.SH EXAMPLES
Count down in twos:
.PP
.EX
$ seq 10 \-2 4
10
8
6
4
.EE
.PP
Number files with a fixed width:
.PP
.EX
$ for i in $(seq \-w 1 10); do touch file$i; done
.EE
.PP
A comma-separated list:
.PP
.EX
$ seq \-s , 1 5
1,2,3,4,5
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR printf (1),
.BR printf (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.B seq
is not in POSIX.
It follows the common interface of GNU coreutils and the BSDs.
